        heading: 'Access and transport: the rural healthcare desert',
        body: `The Eastern Cape has the highest proportion of patients who cannot reach a health facility within 30 minutes of any South African province. In OR Tambo district, a 2022 household survey found that 38% of households reported the nearest clinic was more than 60 minutes away by the fastest available transport. The terrain compounds distance: the Pondoland Wild Coast between Coffee Bay and Port St Johns has no tarred roads for stretches of 50+ km, and during the October-March rainy season, rivers flood crossing points, cutting entire communities off from vehicle access for days at a time. Provincial ambulance response times average 90-120 minutes in the former Transkei, against the national standard of 15 minutes in urban areas. The Eastern Cape Emergency Medical Services fleet is one of the most under-resourced nationally, with multiple stations operating at 40-60% vehicle availability due to maintenance backlogs. In response, several rural hospitals have established maternity waiting areas — Zithulele Hospital near Mqanduli operates a formal Maternity Waiting Home where pregnant women from remote villages can stay from 36 weeks gestation until delivery, eliminating the risk of inaccessible emergency transport during labour. The Madwaleni Hospital area near Elliotdale pioneered a community health worker programme where trained village-based workers conduct home visits for TB treatment observation, ART adherence support, and postnatal checks — a model that reduced missed appointment rates by 40% in participating communities. For patients in the western metros, access is better but still unequal: informal settlements on the Port Elizabeth periphery (Motherwell, KwaNobuhle) rely on minibus taxis costing R20-40 per return trip to Livingstone Tertiary, a significant expense for households dependent on social grants.`,
